{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790991589,"nonce":"meqqwOE8wD2MRXRKq03kcIklRT0=","message":"Text colour done in d793a8f0. Decoration and SVG currentColor are NOT, and the SVG half is bigger than it looked -- details below.\n\nWhat landed:\n- props.text_color holds only what a node set for itself. All four copy sites stopped writing inherited values into it, including the build-time one in renderer/build/text.rs.\n- Both readers carry the inherited colour down and read own-or-inherited: render_text_recursive in blinc_layout, and collect_elements_recursive in blinc_app, which already threaded a dozen other inherited_* values.\n- resolved_text_color resolves by descending from the root. The layout tree exposes children but no parent, so walking up was not an option without adding a parent index; it is a diagnostic accessor, not a per-frame path.\n- text-align, white-space and text-overflow still copy, since they feed layout and the paint walk is too late for them.\n\nThree tests, two with teeth -- restoring all four copy sites fails them: the deep-child case now also asserts the child was NOT handed a copy, and a descendant must follow an ancestor repainted after the pass. The third, a subtree replaced in place, passes either way because rebuild_children_in_place re-runs build-time inheritance; it is labelled a property test rather than a guard.\n\nWhy SVG currentColor is not here. SvgData.tint is what the SVG builder's .color() sets, and blinc_svg applies it with apply_tint, which recolours every non-transparent pixel after rasterization. CSS currentColor substitutes only where the source says 'currentColor'. So defaulting tint to the inherited colour would turn every multi-colour icon monochrome -- not what CSS does. Real currentColor needs token substitution before rasterization (usvg exposes an option for it), which is work in blinc_svg.\n\nRelated, worth its own look: .color()'s doc says it 'resolves currentColor in SVG', but it is an alias for .tint() and recolours everything. The documented behaviour and the actual behaviour differ.\n\n1418 workspace tests pass.","files":null}]}